   "text": "This structure is an ertugliflozin-type bicyclic ketal C-aryl glycoside, but the side chain on the bridgehead carbon is CH(OH)CH3 rather than CH2OH.\n\n**Skeleton**\n- The left ring is a para-disubstituted benzene bearing an ethoxy group (CH3CH2\u2013O\u2013).\n- A CH2 links this ring to a second benzene ring. That ring carries Cl ortho to the CH2 and is bonded to the anomeric carbon of the bicycle.\n- The bicycle is a 2,7-dioxabicyclo[2.2.1]heptane-type ring system.\n  - The anomeric carbon carries the aryl group and two ring oxygens: one is the bridge O, the other is the O of the CH2\u2013O arm.\n  - Going round the six-carbon ring from the anomeric carbon, there are three CH\u2013OH carbons. Then comes the quaternary bridgehead carbon, which carries the CH2\u2013O arm, the bridge O, and the CH(OH)CH3 side chain.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\nI read each centre from the wedges and hashes:\n- Anomeric carbon: the aryl bond is hashed.\n- First CH\u2013OH (next to the anomeric carbon): wedge.\n- Second CH\u2013OH: hash.\n- Third CH\u2013OH: wedge.\n- Bridgehead carbon: the CH2 bond is a wedge.\n- CH(OH)CH3 carbon: the CH3 bond is hashed.\n\nI converted these to @/@@ by working out the neighbour order at each centre. The ring-centre pattern matches the known ertugliflozin SMILES, which is a consistency check.\n\nSMILES: CCOc1ccc(Cc2cc(ccc2Cl)[C@@]34[C@@H]([C@H]([C@@H]([C@@](O3)(CO4)[C@@H](C)O)O)O)O)cc1"
